<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Madurai Nayak dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1529 CE – 1736 CE · capital Madurai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Telugu Nayaka rulers of Madurai who built the great Meenakshi temple complex and ruled most of Tamil country for two centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1529 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishwanatha-nayak">Vishwanatha Nayak</a>, son of a Vijayanagara general, as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> after a rebellion there. He organised the region into some seventy-two palayams held by military chiefs, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palayakkarar">Palaiyakkarars</a>, and his successors ruled the southern Tamil country, becoming independent after Vijayanagara's decline.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirumala-nayak">Tirumala Nayak</a>, in the 17th century, rebuilt much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meenakshi-temple">Meenakshi Amman temple</a> and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirumalai-nayak-palace">Tirumalai Nayak palace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaks</a> controlled the pearl fishery coast, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionaries worked in their realm, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roberto-de-nobili">Roberto de Nobili</a> at Madurai. Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rani-mangammal">Mangammal</a>, regent around 1700, built roads, rest houses and tanks. The court used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telugu-language">Telugu</a> while Tamil culture flourished.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijaya-ranga-chokkanatha">Vijaya Ranga Chokkanatha</a> died in 1731, his widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meenakshi-of-madurai">Meenakshi</a> became regent amid disputes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanda-sahib">Chanda Sahib</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/carnatic-sultanate">Carnatic Sultanate</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> in 1736, ending the dynasty. The region passed to the Nawabs and then the British.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/madurai-nayak-dynasty.jpg" alt="Madurai Nayak dynasty" width="320" height="427"><figcaption>Image: SPK · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thirumalai-Nayak-Palace-Madurai.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1529 CE – 1736 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Madurai</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vishwanatha Nayak, Tirumala Nayak, Rani Mangammal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Telugu, Tamil</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Madurai Nayaks, Nayaks of Madurai</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1632&amp;focus=madurai-nayak-dynasty" aria-label="Madurai Nayak dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6834 1807 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Madurai Nayak dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7154 2063L7135 2099M7154 2063L7088 1864M7154 2063L7177 1953M7154 2063L7180 2031M7154 2063L7176 2083"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7135 2099h0M7088 1864h0M7177 1953h0M7180 2031h0M7176 2083h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7154 2063h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7154 2063h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Madurai. <a href="/atlas?year=1632&amp;focus=madurai-nayak-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

